---
import AiTextDisclosure from '../../../../components/editorial/AiTextDisclosure.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../../../styles/explorables.css';
import '../../../../styles/relational-design.css';
const start = String.raw`docker run --name staycurrent-practice --rm -d -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=practice-only postgres:18`;
const ready = String.raw`docker exec staycurrent-practice pg_isready -h localhost -U postgres`;
const create = String.raw`docker exec staycurrent-practice createdb -U postgres staycurrent_workshop_1
docker exec -it staycurrent-practice psql -X -U postgres -d staycurrent_workshop_1`;
const check = String.raw`SELECT current_database();
\pset null 'SQL NULL'`;
const next = String.raw`docker exec staycurrent-practice createdb -U postgres staycurrent_workshop_2
docker exec -it staycurrent-practice psql -X -U postgres -d staycurrent_workshop_2`;
const second = String.raw`docker exec -it staycurrent-practice psql -X -U postgres -d staycurrent_workshop_1`;
---
<Base title="Start the SQL practice" description="Open a disposable PostgreSQL database and choose the independent setup supplied by each relational article.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
    <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Start the SQL practice</h1><p>Run an article’s SQL in a database of your own.</p><AiTextDisclosure /></header>
    <p>The relational articles explain their results without requiring a database. Running the SQL lets you change a record or a query and test your own prediction. This guide opens a disposable PostgreSQL instance, then gives each article a fresh database.</p>
    <p>The articles follow one tea shop, but each downloadable experiment supplies its own setup and states what it checks. Use that article’s instructions rather than loading an earlier example first. Some downloads require a specific disposable server, account or cluster; a general practice database does not satisfy those guards.</p>
    <p>For a first experiment without installing anything, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/">open the SQL sandbox</a>. Each guided lesson prepares its own records. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/">relational introduction</a> follows the shop purchase through its tables and transactions.</p>
    <section id="start">
      <h2>Open a local database</h2>
      <p>This route uses Docker and PostgreSQL 18. Docker runs the database in a <strong>container</strong>, a separate environment on your computer; the image supplies PostgreSQL and its <code>psql</code> query client. Have Docker installed and running first. On macOS or Windows, open Docker Desktop and wait for its engine to start; the <a href="https://docs.docker.com/get-started/get-docker/">official installation guide</a> covers installation. On Linux, use a running Docker Engine.</p>
      <p>Run this in your computer’s terminal. The first run downloads the PostgreSQL image. It starts a disposable container called <code>staycurrent-practice</code>, with no database port published to your computer. The password belongs only to this practice instance.</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Terminal command to start the practice PostgreSQL container"><Code code={start} lang="bash" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <p>Check that startup has finished:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Terminal command to check database readiness"><Code code={ready} lang="bash" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <p>Continue when it reports <code>localhost:5432 - accepting connections</code>. Otherwise, wait briefly and check again. If Docker cannot connect to its daemon, its engine is not ready. If the container name is already in use, keep your existing practice instance or choose another name consistently in these commands.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="first-query">
      <h2>Create a fresh database for the article</h2>
      <p>In your terminal, create an empty database and open psql:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Terminal commands to create and enter an article practice database"><Code code={create} lang="bash" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <p>The prompt changes to <code>staycurrent_workshop_1=#</code>. You are now talking to PostgreSQL. Enter these commands there:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="psql commands to check the database and display missing values"><Code code={check} lang="sql" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <p>The query should return <code>staycurrent_workshop_1</code>. <code>\pset null</code> makes missing SQL values display as “SQL NULL”; it does not change the records. Commands beginning with a backslash belong to psql and do not need a semicolon. SQL statements such as <code>SELECT</code> do.</p>
      <p>Follow the chosen download’s instructions and compare each result before trying a variation. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">joins article</a> includes a data-and-queries download using temporary tables. Other articles supply guarded verifiers that require their own isolated fixture rather than this general practice database.</p>
      <p>A statement rejected by a constraint may be the intended result. Inspect the error before continuing. Inside an aborted transaction, enter <code>ROLLBACK;</code>, then resume from the article’s stated next step. If setup fails unexpectedly, stop: later statements cannot be assumed to have succeeded.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="article-setups">
      <h2>Start the next article in another empty database</h2>
      <p>Enter <code>\q</code> in psql to return to your terminal. Create another database in the same container:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Terminal commands to create a fresh database for the next article"><Code code={next} lang="bash" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <p>Use the next article’s own setup here. Choose another new name for each fresh attempt. This keeps an earlier table, changed record or open transaction from changing the result you are trying to explain.</p>
      <p>When a schedule needs clients A and B, both must connect to the <em>same</em> article database. To add a second connection to the first example, open another terminal and run:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Terminal command to open a second client to the first article database"><Code code={second} lang="bash" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <p>Follow the schedule’s client labels rather than sending every statement on one connection. End both transactions before starting again. Run <code>VACUUM</code> by itself outside a transaction when an example calls for it.</p>
      <p>SQLite examples use their separately labelled setup, sometimes an in-memory database created by application code. Use that setup for those examples; PostgreSQL and SQLite syntax is not interchangeable.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="further-practice">
      <h2>Use the native worksheets for operating experiments</h2>
      <p>A crash, restore or replica experiment needs control of a server and its storage, beyond creating another database. The product branches supply their own isolated environments and list their required binaries, versions and ports:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/backup-and-restore/">PostgreSQL restore article</a> explains its isolated recovery rehearsal, the observed result and the limits of that evidence.</li>
        <li>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/cockroachdb/keys-and-ranges/">CockroachDB branch</a> explains the native observations and requirements for its cluster experiments.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/upgrades/#rehearse-the-transfer-mode">The PostgreSQL major-upgrade rehearsal</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/cockroachdb/upgrades/">CockroachDB rolling-upgrade rehearsal</a> have their own version requirements.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Follow each worksheet’s setup and cleanup instructions. These experiments own their temporary servers; they do not run inside the Docker database above. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/#lessons">browser lesson catalogue</a> remains another way to explore the ideas, with independent fixtures and explicitly labelled models where a real server operation is unavailable.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="finish">
      <h2>Finish the practice</h2>
      <p>Leave each psql session with <code>\q</code>, then run <code>docker stop staycurrent-practice</code> in your terminal. Because this disposable instance was started with <code>--rm</code>, stopping it removes the container and all its practice databases. Start a new instance for another session.</p>
      <details class="sql-setup"><summary>If you already have PostgreSQL and psql</summary><p>Use a new empty practice database, then open <code>psql -X -d your_practice_database</code>. Follow your chosen download’s setup and stated requirements. Connection host, account and authentication depend on your installation; the Docker route above supplies one local setup.</p></details>
    </section>
    <nav class="series-reading" aria-label="Continue reading"><p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/modelling/">Relational data modelling →</a><span>Choose what the shop records mean, then test those choices.</span></p><p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/#collection">Return to the collection</a></p></nav>
    <section class="essay-sources"><h2>Sources and further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://hub.docker.com/_/postgres">Official PostgreSQL container image</a>: startup configuration and the included database client.</li><li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-psql.html">PostgreSQL 18: psql</a>: interactive queries, file scripts and session commands.</li></ul></section>
  </article>
</Base>
